  "body": "I am testing open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object. Its documented rule says: \"The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text.\" Reply A is the text '{\"top\": 1} and then [{\"a\":1}, {\"b\":2}]'. Reply B is the text '{\"b\":2}'. For Reply A, the rule requires the answer {\"top\": 1}, because the only top-level object in that text is the first object; the objects {\"a\":1} and {\"b\":2} sit inside a JSON array and are therefore not top-level objects. For Reply B, the rule requires the answer {\"b\":2}, because that is the last top-level object. The check, however, returns {\"b\":2} for both replies: in Reply A its top-level scan finds every balanced object starting with a left brace, including the two objects inside the array, and then takes the last one, which is {\"b\":2}. Thus the rule requires the check to tell the two replies apart, but the check gives the same answer. I would be wrong if the documented rule's phrase \"top-level object\" is meant to include objects inside arrays, so that for Reply A the last top-level object is also {\"b\":2}; in that case the check's answer would match the rule for both replies and there would be no failure to tell them apart.",
